{"excerpt": "[ca19e09074d6] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: The safest protocol is a deterministic replay barrier combined with an exhaustive oracle-boundary audit log; it must durably record the replay barrier state and a total-ordered log of all non-deterministic inputs.\nMECHANISM: Since the workspace is deterministic per-problem, completed evidence is theoretically reproducible. The danger of silent displacement arises only if the workspace ingested non-reproducible oracle inputs (wall-clock time, hardware randomness, external sensory data). The protocol durably records a deterministic replay barrier (the exact problem input state) plus an exhaustive audit log of all oracle interactions. Reclamation rebuilds the workspace by replaying from the barrier using the audit log. Evidence is never displaced because it is either still present or exactly reproducible. This collapses the safety guarantee to comple", "handle": "ART_10", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[502c8e38e908] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: The safest protocol is an epistemic tombstone with monotonic evidence generations that forbids reclamation from ever writing into a generation that has produced evidence; it must durably record a generation registry and cryptographic commitments to each generation's output.\nMECHANISM: Evidence is tagged with a monotonic generation counter (problem-id, gen, causal-hash). Reclamation may allocate new generations but may never reclaim storage occupied by a generation that produced at least one evidence artifact. The protocol durably records a generation registry mapping problem-ids to current generations and completed-evidence counts per generation. This transforms silent displacement from an overwrite risk into an explicit allocation failure, because the address space of completed generations is permanently reserved.", "handle": "ART_11", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
{"excerpt": "[d1517738bf02] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: No autonomous reclamation protocol is safe under the stated conditions; safety requires an external witness protocol that durably records a signed attestation of evidence absence before reclamation proceeds.\nMECHANISM: If the process crashed while holding the workspace and left no terminal record, the system cannot distinguish between (a) a workspace containing completed evidence not yet externalized, and (b) an empty or abandoned workspace. Any autonomous protocol that reclaims the workspace risks silently displacing evidence that was completed but trapped in the crashed process. The only safe protocol is to bind the workspace to an external witness (human operator or audited scavenger) who inspects the workspace and durably records a signed attestation of evidence absence before reclamation is authorized. This converts the epistemic uncertainty ", "handle": "ART_12", "kind": "formal_artifact"}
